<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Menahem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 752–742 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Menahem of Israel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Menahem was king of Israel from about 752 to 742 BCE. He seized the throne by killing Shallum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> and, according to the Books of Kings, paid the Assyrian king 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, raised by a levy of fifty shekels on each wealthy man, to secure his rule. Assyrian annals also list him among the tributaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menahem.jpg" alt="Menahem" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guillaume Rouille · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menahem.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
